What was said, kept
===================

The history module stores the messages this server delivers and, later,
hands them back.  It is phase 2 of doc/proposals/006-comunicaciones-
unificadas.md, and this file is what an operator needs to run it.

It is one module, modules/services/history/, over PostgreSQL through
include/db.h.  It owns its schema and ships it as migrations, so it is a
directory module and an operator applies them by hand:

  /MODULE MIGRATION APPLY history

Nothing is stored until that has been done.  A server whose schema is
missing logs one error a minute and delivers messages exactly as before;
history is a thing that is added to a network, never a thing whose
absence breaks one.


WHAT IT NEEDS
=============

  Module { name = "postgres"; };        the driver
  Module { name = "history"; };         this
  Database { dsn = "..."; pool = 4; };  where

and two features, on every server of the network:

  NETWORK_FEATURES   carries msgid over P10
  NETWORK_TIME       carries the time a message was sent over P10

Both are what make one message one row -- see TOPOLOGY below.  The module
says so in the log at load time if either is off; it still runs, and the
result is one copy of every message per server, which is not something
anybody notices until they read the history back.

Worker threads are not optional in practice: the database driver is built
on them (WORKER_THREADS, doc/readme.workers).


WHAT IS STORED
==============

Channel messages, always.  PRIVMSG and NOTICE alike, from a local client
or from a link.

Direct messages, when HISTORY_PRIVATE is on (it is by default) **and both
ends have identified**.  The second condition is not a judgement about
guests.  A direct message is shown back to the two people in it and to
nobody else, and the only durable handle on a person this server has is
the account the network's services say they are logged in to: filing a
message under a bare nickname would show it to whoever is wearing that
nickname next week.  With nobody it can safely be shown to, there is no
reason to keep it.

Reactions, as TAGMSGs.  A TAGMSG carries tags and no text of its own, so
most of them are nothing to keep -- a typing indicator is true for four
seconds and then is not, and a row saying somebody was typing in March is
not history.  A reaction is different, and not really an exception: it has
its own identifier, its own time, and it says something about a message
that is still there.  So it is stored as a message -- kind 2, the reaction
in the body, and reply_to naming what it is about -- which is what makes
removing one and reading them back in order the same operations as for
anything else, and what keeps a message with four hundred reactions from
being a four-hundred-element array nobody can index.

Never stored:

  - Anything sent to a service (+S or +k).  That is where IDENTIFY,
    REGISTER and DROP send a password.  The core does not even report
    those to the module; see HOOK_MESSAGE_DELIVERED in
    doc/readme.modules.
  - Masked messages ($#mask, $host).  An operator addressing a whole
    server is an announcement, not a conversation, and there is no target
    to file it under.
  - CTCP that is not an ACTION.  A VERSION, a PING or a DCC handshake is
    two clients talking about themselves.
  - TAGMSG.  It carries tags and no text, and tags are phase 3.


TOPOLOGY: EVERY SERVER WRITES WHAT IT DELIVERS
==============================================

There is no designated archivist.  A designated archivist is a server
whose split takes the record with it, and a network that has to agree on
which server that is.

Instead, every server that relays a message writes it, and the database
throws away the copies.  That works because the two things identifying a
row travel with the message rather than being invented by whichever
server is handling it:

  msgid   the name the whole network calls this message by
  time    when its origin server said it was sent

So the second, third and fourth writer are all inserting the same row,
and the insert is written to do nothing when it is already there.  This
is why the two features above are not optional: without msgid each server
invents its own name, and without time each server records its own clock
reading, and either one makes the rows different.


THE SCHEMA
==========

One table, message, partitioned by month.

A partitioned table in PostgreSQL will not take a unique constraint that
does not include the partition key, so the key of a message is
(sent_at, msgid) rather than msgid alone.  That is not a workaround --
it is the same statement as the paragraph above, written down: two
servers only agree on the key if they agree on the timestamp.

  sent_at            when, from the message
  msgid              what the network calls it
  kind               0 PRIVMSG, 1 NOTICE, 2 TAGMSG
  is_channel         whether target is a channel
  target             the channel or nickname as the sender wrote it
  target_canon       lower-cased the way IRC lower-cases a name
  sender_nick        the nickname they were wearing
  sender_account     the account they were logged in to, canonical, or NULL
  recipient_account  for a direct message; NULL for a channel
  body               the text, or the reaction for a kind-2 row
  reply_to           the message this one answers, or NULL
  edited_at          when the author last rewrote it, or NULL
  body_search        tsvector over body, maintained by the database

Three indexes do the reading: BRIN over sent_at (the rows inside one
month arrive in time order, which is the case BRIN exists for), a
(target_canon, sent_at DESC) index for a channel, and a pair of them for
a conversation -- two, because either end may be the sender and the query
is an OR.  GIN over body_search does the searching.

'simple' is the text-search configuration, not a language: the server
does not know what language a channel speaks, and stemming the wrong one
is worse than not stemming at all.

Partitions are created ahead of time, for this month and next, at load
and every six hours -- every five minutes until the schema answers, since
a module is loaded from ircd.conf and its migrations are applied by an
operator some time afterwards.  A message that lands outside every
partition goes to message_default rather than being refused: a safety net,
not a place to keep things.

The net needed a floor under it.  PostgreSQL will not create a partition
whose range the default already holds rows for, so every message stored
before the schema was migrated permanently blocked its own month, and
that month's traffic stayed in the default for ever -- where retention
cannot drop it as a partition and the time-ordered locality BRIN depends
on is gone.  Since v3 of the schema, history_ensure_partition() moves
those rows out of the default, creates the partition and puts them back,
all inside the one statement it is called by.


THREADS AND REACTIONS
=====================

Both are the same tag pointing at the same thing.  +draft/reply says
"this follows that"; a reaction is a TAGMSG carrying +draft/react as well,
which says "this is about that".  The identifier they point at is the
msgid the whole network already agrees on, so nothing new had to be
invented for either.

The tag is the client's claim and is kept verbatim.  "This is a reply to
that" is exactly the sort of thing only the person writing it can say, and
the server has no way to be more right about it.  There is no foreign key
on reply_to for the same reason: a message may reply to one the retention
already dropped, or to one from a server this one never heard from.

They are stored only if CLIENTTAGDENY allows them -- a tag the network
refuses to carry is not one to write down either -- and they go back out
the same way, under whatever the policy is at the time of the read rather
than at the time of the write.

READING IT BACK: CHATHISTORY
============================

The IRCv3 command, in all six of its shapes:

  CHATHISTORY LATEST  <target> <*|point> <limit>
  CHATHISTORY BEFORE  <target> <point> <limit>
  CHATHISTORY AFTER   <target> <point> <limit>
  CHATHISTORY AROUND  <target> <point> <limit>
  CHATHISTORY BETWEEN <target> <point> <point> <limit>
  CHATHISTORY TARGETS <point> <point> <limit>

A point is timestamp=<ISO 8601>, msgid=<id>, or * for "from the end".
BETWEEN's two points may be given in either order; which is earlier is
decided by the database, because it is the thing comparing them.

The answer is a batch of type "chathistory" whose parameter is the
target, holding the messages oldest first -- whichever end they were
asked for from -- as the PRIVMSGs and NOTICEs they were, with the prefix
that was stored and the @msgid and @time they already had.  That last
part is the point: a client is shown the same message everybody else saw,
and can put it in order against what it already has.  An empty answer is
an empty batch, not silence: "there is nothing there" is a different
thing from "the server ignored you".

TARGETS lists conversations, not channels: a client already knows which
channels it is on, and the ones it is not on it may not read.  Each one
is a line of the form

  CHATHISTORY TARGETS <nickname> <when it was last spoken to>

inside a batch of type draft/chathistory-targets.

The client needs the batch capability.  Without it the messages would
arrive loose and indistinguishable from the ones happening now, which is
worse than no answer, so the request is refused -- and a client with no
standard-replies is refused in words, as a NOTICE, rather than with a
FAIL it could not read.

Errors are FAIL CHATHISTORY, with the codes the specification names:
UNKNOWN_COMMAND for a subcommand that is not one of the six,
INVALID_PARAMS for a selector that is not one of the three forms or a
subcommand with nowhere to start from, INVALID_TARGET for a channel the
client is not on or a conversation it is not in, and MESSAGE_ERROR when
the store could not answer.

The capability's value is the largest answer this server will give
(HISTORY_MAX_LIMIT), so a client knows the ceiling before it asks rather
than by having its limit quietly cut down:

  draft/chathistory=100

WHO MAY READ WHAT
=================

A channel: whoever is on it.  A channel's history is exactly as private
as the channel, and somebody who is not in it was not there for the
conversation either.

A conversation: the two people in it, by account.  The query is on the
pair of accounts, so a third party asking about somebody else's
conversation gets an empty answer rather than a refusal -- a refusal with
a different shape would itself say the conversation exists.  A client
that has not identified has no account, so it has no conversations, and
is told so.

An asynchronous note: the answer comes back from a database a round trip
later, so a client that labelled the command (IRCv3 labeled-response)
gets its ACK straight away and the batch afterwards, outside the label.
That is the honest shape of an asynchronous answer; the batch names its
target and identifies itself well enough to be matched without one.


FINDING SOMETHING: SEARCH
=========================

  SEARCH <target|*> [from=<nick>] [after=<time>] [before=<time>]
                    [limit=<n>] :<what to look for>

The GIN index has been in the schema since v1 and this is what uses it.
There is no IRCv3 specification for searching -- the protocol has never
had a word for it -- so the command and its capability are this server's
own (blacknode/search).  What comes back is an ordinary batch of ordinary
messages, replayed exactly the way CHATHISTORY replays them, so a client
that can read one can read this; the capability says the command exists,
not that the answer needs anything new.

**What may be searched is what may be read, asked at the moment it is
asked.**  A channel this connection is on now, a conversation it is in,
or `*` for both at once.  There is no scope that means the network, and a
client with no channels and no account is told there is nowhere to look
rather than handed an empty answer that reads as "nothing matched".  A
channel somebody is not on is refused in words; a `*` search simply does
not reach it, which is the same answer for the same reason a stranger is
not told what is in a channel they cannot see.

Each result carries the target it was *sent* to and not the one that was
searched: a `*` search answers with messages from several places, and a
client has to be able to tell which is which.  They arrive oldest first,
inside a `blacknode/search` batch, under the names and times they always
had.

**What to look for is the last parameter**, not a `key=value` like the
rest, because it is the one thing here that is a sentence -- as the
trailing parameter it may hold spaces, quotes and anything else somebody
types, which is where IRC has put the free-form argument since PRIVMSG.

It is `websearch_to_tsquery` underneath and not `to_tsquery`: quotes make
a phrase, `or` is a choice, a leading `-` excludes, and nothing a person
can type into a box makes it fail.  `to_tsquery` raises an error on an
apostrophe, a stray bracket or two words with no operator between them,
and a search box that can be made to error is a search box that will be.

The text is the first condition in the WHERE clause, always.  It is the
only one the index can answer; narrowing by channel first would read
every message that channel has ever had and test each one, which is the
difference between a search and a table scan.


CHANGING A MESSAGE: EDIT
========================

  EDIT <target> <msgid> :<new text>

The other half of REDACT, here for the same reason: **you cannot edit
what nobody stored**.  A server with no history has no copy to change and
no way to know who wrote the message being pointed at.  Nothing is
relayed until the row has been updated -- a client showing the new text
while the store still holds the old one is the one outcome worse than not
having this.

**The identifier does not change**, which is the whole reason an edit is
not simply another message.  The replies and the reactions point at it,
the read markers are ordered against it, and a message that was edited is
still the message they are about.

**Only the author, ever.**  A channel operator may REDACT, because
moderating is taking something out of a room; nobody may EDIT somebody
else's message, because that is putting words in their mouth under their
own prefix, and there is no version of that which is moderation.  Not an
operator with history_admin either -- they can delete the message, which
leaves a hole everybody can see.

HISTORY_EDIT_WINDOW is how long the author has, and it is shorter than
the redaction window on purpose: a redaction leaves a hole, while a
change leaves a sentence nobody can tell was ever different, so the time
in which that can happen is the time in which somebody is fixing a typo.
0 is for ever.

An empty new text is refused rather than treated as a deletion.  There is
a command for that and this is not it -- and an edit that emptied a
message would be a redaction that left the reactions and the replies
pointing at nothing.  A TAGMSG is refused too: what a reaction carries
*is* the reaction, so there is no text in it to rewrite; taking one back
and adding another is what changing a reaction is.

The target named has to be the one the message was sent to, for the
reason REDACT checks the same thing.  The refusals are FAIL EDIT with
UNKNOWN_TARGET, UNKNOWN_MSGID, EDIT_FORBIDDEN and EDIT_FAILED.

Live, a change reaches only clients that asked for
blacknode/message-edit, the way a redaction does.  It crosses a link --
P10 token ED -- because every server's clients were shown the message;
what arrives from a peer is an announcement, since the store it would
have checked is the same one.

Read back afterwards, the message carries **blacknode/edited** with the
time it was last changed.  That is a *server* tag, with no leading `+`:
it is what the store says about the message rather than something the
message carried, nobody claimed it when it was sent, and no client may
claim it about somebody else's message -- so CLIENTTAGDENY has no say in
it, the way it has none over batch.


TAKING A MESSAGE BACK: REDACT
=============================

  REDACT <target> <msgid> [:<reason>]

IRCv3 draft/message-redaction, and it lives in this module rather than in
the core for one reason: **you cannot redact what nobody stored**.  A
server with no history has nothing to delete and no way to know who wrote
the message being pointed at, so the command would be an announcement that
something was withdrawn with nothing withdrawn.

That is also what makes the policy checkable.  Whether somebody may take a
message back depends on who wrote it, and the only thing that knows is the
row -- so the answer is a database round trip away, and the command waits
for it.  **Nothing is relayed until the deletion has happened**: a client
that removed the message from its view while the store kept it would be
the one outcome worse than not supporting this at all.

Who may:

  - the person who wrote it, within HISTORY_REDACT_WINDOW seconds (0
    means for ever);
  - anybody with ops on the channel it was said in, with no window,
    because moderating is not undoing and a moderator who had to be quick
    about it would not be one;
  - an operator with history_admin, for the same reason they can delete an
    account's whole record.

A direct message is the first case only.  There is nobody with ops on a
conversation.

The target named has to be the one the message was actually sent to.
Otherwise naming any channel you have ops on would let you delete a
message from any other.

Redacting a message takes the reactions to it with it -- a reaction to a
message that is gone is a reference to nothing.  It does **not** take the
replies: a reply is a message of its own, somebody else said it, and
redacting one message is not permission to redact the conversation that
followed.  Their reply_to is left pointing at a message that is no longer
there, which is exactly what happened.

It reaches only clients that asked for draft/message-redaction.  To
anybody else REDACT is a command they have never heard of, and a line they
cannot parse is not an improvement on a message they still have; what they
keep is what they were shown, which is the honest outcome for a client
that does not implement this.  The refusals are FAIL REDACT with the
specification's codes: UNKNOWN_TARGET, UNKNOWN_MSGID, REDACT_FORBIDDEN and
REDACT_FAILED.

It is the one thing here that crosses a link -- P10 token RD -- because
every server's clients were shown the message.  What arrives from another
server is an announcement: the deletion has happened already, every server
reads the same store, and there is nothing left to check.


HOW FAR SOMEBODY HAS READ: MARKREAD
===================================

  MARKREAD <target>                       what is my marker?
  MARKREAD <target> timestamp=<ISO 8601>  move it here
  MARKREAD <target> *                     I have read nothing

and the answer to all three is the same line, saying where the marker
*is*:

  MARKREAD <target> timestamp=<ISO 8601>
  MARKREAD <target> *

IRCv3 draft/read-marker.  It is what turns a history into an inbox:
without it a client reconnecting can fetch everything it missed but cannot
tell which of it it has already seen, so either everything is unread or
nothing is.

**Per account, not per connection.**  A person reads on their phone and
expects their laptop to know, which is the whole point.  A client with no
account has no person for this to belong to and is told so
(FAIL MARKREAD NEED_REGISTRATION).

**It only moves forward.**  Two clients of the same person race
constantly -- one is catching up while the other is at the bottom -- and a
marker that could move backwards would make messages unread again every
time the slower one reported in.  So a request to move it back is not an
error: the answer is where the marker is, which is where it already was.

Moving it tells **every one of that person's clients**, not only the one
that moved it, because that is how a laptop finds out what a phone did.
Asking answers the client that asked.

The marker is a timestamp and not a msgid.  "Everything up to here" is a
point in time, and a message arriving late -- from a server that was
split -- is behind the marker if it was *sent* behind it, which is the
answer a person means.  A msgid would name one message and say nothing
about the ones around it.

It never crosses a link: the marker lives in the store every server
reads, so the other servers' copies of that person are told by their own
server.


RETENTION, PURGING, EXPORT AND DELETION
=======================================

An operator does all four through one command, and they are all the same
thing seen from different sides -- what is kept, for how long, how it is
handed over, and how it is destroyed:

  HISTORY STATUS [<account>]   how much there is
  HISTORY PURGE                apply the retention now
  HISTORY EXPORT <account>     write out everything about one person
  HISTORY FORGET <account>     and delete it

All four need `history_admin` in the Operator{} block.  It is not a
default for anybody, which is deliberate: it is the difference between an
operator and somebody who can read every conversation on the network, and
that has to be written down rather than arrived at by being an operator at
all.

HISTORY_RETENTION is how many days are kept; 0 keeps everything.  Every
six hours the module drops whole monthly partitions that end before the
cutoff, which costs nothing whatever they hold, and deletes what is left
straddling it.  PURGE runs that pass now.

EXPORT and FORGET read and delete by the same predicate -- every message
the account sent and every direct message it received -- and that is the
point.  What a person is handed has to be what a person can have
destroyed, or one of the two is lying about what "their data" means.

A direct message is one row, not two, so forgetting an account takes the
other end's copy of that conversation with it.  That is the answer being
asked for and not a side effect: what is being deleted is the message,
and there is only one of it.  The delete is logged at NOTICE with the
count and the account, because which operator destroyed somebody's record
is the part nobody can reconstruct afterwards.

The export is a file, not something sent over IRC: what it is for is a
request that arrives by post and is answered by post, and an operator
hands the file over.  It goes to HISTORY_EXPORT_DIR, which is empty by
default -- a server writes no files until it has been told where to.  The
name is the account and the moment, the mode is 0600, and it is never
written over an existing file.

The format is JSON Lines, one message per line:

  {"time":"2026-09-16T15:43:52.000Z","msgid":"AB8aqtoa5z9","kind":0,
   "channel":false,"target":"ben","from":"ana!ana@xxxxxx.yyyyyy.v4",
   "from_account":"ana","to_account":"ben","text":"a private one"}

It is read in pages of five hundred, by keyset -- everything after the
last row handed over -- rather than by offset, so a long export is a
series of index range scans and never re-reads what it has already
written.  There is no cursor held open: db.h has no transactions, and a
pooled connection is not the caller's to keep.  One export runs at a
time; a second is told to wait.


FEATURES
========

  HISTORY_RETENTION   days to keep, 0 for forever      (default 90)
  HISTORY_PRIVATE     store direct messages            (default TRUE)
  HISTORY_MAX_LIMIT   cap on one CHATHISTORY request   (default 100)
  HISTORY_EXPORT_DIR  where HISTORY EXPORT writes      (default none)
  HISTORY_REDACT_WINDOW  seconds to take back your own (default 3600)
  HISTORY_EDIT_WINDOW    seconds to rewrite your own    (default 900)

See doc/readme.features.


HOW IT IS PUT TOGETHER
======================

  history.c        the module: the hook, the command, the capability, the
                   maintenance timer, and the warnings an operator needs
                   before the first user does
  hist_admin.c     /HISTORY: what an operator does about what is stored
  hist_capture.c   which of what the server delivers is a conversation,
                   and which of its tags are part of the message
  hist_read.c      what a client asked for, whether it may see it, and
                   how the answer goes on the wire
  hist_redact.c    REDACT: who may take a message back, and what goes
                   with it
  hist_edit.c      EDIT: who may change one, which is only ever its author
  hist_search.c    SEARCH: what may be looked through, and how a person's
                   words become a query
  hist_marker.c    MARKREAD: how far somebody has read
  hist_store.c     every statement this module sends, and nothing else
  hist_store.h     the store as the rest of the module sees it
  migrations/      the schema

The split between the last two is the point.  The code that answers a
client has no business knowing SQL, and retention, purging and deletion
by account need one place where they happen rather than a DELETE in
whichever file first needed one.  It is not a door left open for a second
database engine: proposal 006 section 7.1 closes that question.

Nothing here blocks.  Every statement goes out through db.h, which hands
it to the driver's own threads and calls back in the main thread; a
database that is slow or down costs nothing but the rows it did not
store, and the failures are logged at most once a minute because a
database that is down fails one insert per message on the whole network.
